import Mathlib

namespace Erdos997

/-
# Problem Description

Erdős Problem 997. For every real `α`, the sequence `{α p_n}` of fractional parts along the
primes is not well distributed. `erdos_997` proves this.

`erdos_997` is proved unconditionally here: the Maynard--Tao theorem in the form of Banks,
Freiberg and Turnage-Butterbaugh, previously assumed in this repository as
`maynardTaoBFT`, is now a theorem — a special case of `MaynardBFT.consecutive_primes` from
the vendored `Util.MaynardTao` development. Its statement is kept exactly as this
repository had it. The argument for the problem itself is unchanged: `maynardTaoBFT`
supplies runs of consecutive primes in a common residue class with bounded total gap,
which forces clustering of `{α p_n}` on the circle and so defeats well-distribution.

The Maynard--Tao development is by plby (github.com/plby/lean-proofs), `src/latest/Util/
MaynardTao`, reached through `src/latest/ErdosProblems/Erdos997.lean`; it rests on the
`BoundedGaps` library of frenzymath/FormalPantheon.

Flattened single-file vendoring of the 671-module import closure of `Util.MaynardTao` (575
of them from `BoundedGaps`), in dependency order, with project-internal imports removed so
that `Mathlib` is the only import, each module wrapped in its own `section` with any
end-of-file scopes closed explicitly. Declarations keep their upstream names. One module,
`Util/Density.lean`, declares into Mathlib's own `Set` namespace, so it is emitted with
explicit `_root_.Set.` prefixes rather than nested. Names declared in more than one
upstream module — legal there because they are `private` and so module-scoped, except one
that is public in a single declarer — are suffixed `_v1`/`_v2`/... here, with the public
declarer keeping the bare name. Upstream `#print axioms` lines are dropped in favour of the
single one below. No mathematical content is changed.
-/

